\f1\fs22 When she arrived at the Ironing Room, Chip and Johnny in high opera, shouted insults at each other. Dr Ortolan was standing outside spitting and arm swinging range, apparently working through a checklist on his tablet. The few junior techs who had turned up were hunched at their stations, examining the fingerprints on their screens. One was playing a game with the sound muted. Another wore ear pods and was rocking along to music. Sarah suspected it was Yacht Metal\'97the girl had been playing a compilation called \'93Spandex, Divorce & Gangrene\'94 for weeks.\
When Chip saw Sarah arrive, he went straight to the overjoyed end of the relieved spectrum.\
\'93Sarah!\'94 Johnny double-took at her appearance. \'93Shouldn\'92t you be resting?\'94\
Sarah took the seat at the Ironing Room command console. Johnny\'92s bottom lip quivered.\
\'93What\'92s going on?\'94 Sarah asked. \'93Why aren\'92t you all at home waiting for Santa to arrive?\'94\
\'93The Washing Machine woke up,\'94 Johnny said.\
Sarah turned to Ortolan. \'93Woke up?\'94\
\'93It became active,\'94 Ortolan said and checked his watch. \'93About seventy minutes ago.\'94\
Sarah tapped and swiped her console. A live feed of the Laundry brightened the room from the front wall. If the Washing Machine was active, it was not operating. \'93There\'92s no monkey business,\'94 she said.\
\'93They\'92re waiting,\'94 Johnny said.\
Sarah thought he was oddly self-confident. He wasn\'92t sweating. \'93Who are waiting?\'94 she asked.\
\'93Hackers,\'94 Chip said, losing patience.\
\'93Inter-universal researchers,\'94 Johnny said. \'93Like us.\'94\
\'93Apparently, the Sages held a conversation with versions of themselves from another universe.\'94 No one missed Chip\'92s sarcastic tone, except for the tech who was possibly not yet halfway through \'93Spandex, Divorce & Gangrene\'94.\
\'93We\'92ve missed quite a bit,\'94 Chip said.\
Sarah let that soak in. \'93Go on.\'94\
\'93Mr Stoat thought he was subscribing to a new version of the Washing Machine software. GygesOS Daintree version 42.42.42,\'94 Chip mocked.\
Sarah stared at Johnny. \'93You downloaded malware?\'94 she hissed.\
Ortolan held up his hand. \'93To be fair, I was the first to arrive this morning, and the download had already completed.\'94\
\'93But Stoat went ahead and completed the subscription process anyway,\'94 Chip accused.\
Ortolan dropped his hand and scratched his mouth. \'93I already tried to reload from a backup.\'94\
\'93What?\'94 Johnny was indignant. \'93On whose authority?\'94\
\'93But the new version blocked me.\'94\
\'93See? I had no choice,\'94 Johnny tacked. \
\'93See?\'94 Chip countered. \'93We\'92ve been hacked.\'94\
\'93If it\'92s a hack, it\'92s not from this universe,\'94 Johnny said smugly. \'93Same as the missing socks message: no source from within the known universe.\'94 Sarah noted he still wasn\'92t sweating.\
Ortolan continued. \'93It gave me a long lecture about contravening several multi-universal accords, complete with document titles and broken links.\'94 He flicked the face of his tablet, and everyone else\'92s tablet acknowledged. Johnny\'92s played the chorus of \'93Barbie Girl\'94, by Aqua. It was excruciating.\
\'93Did it cause the seismic activity?\'94 Sarah asked. \
\'93Don\'92t know either way yet,\'94 Chip said.\
\'93So what now?\'94\
\'93Swing them monkeys,\'94 Johnny answered.\
\'93Replace all the chips and solid-state memory in the hardware,\'94 Chip overrode.\
Johnny smirked at Chip. \'93Might I remind you that Eloise threw you in the brig.\'94 He looked at Sarah, minus the smirk. \'93And you are on maternity leave. I think we can all agree that pregnancy is not a good look in the workplace.\'94\
\'93We just saved Eloise\'92s life,\'94 Sarah said firmly.\
\'93Oh, you think doing your employer favours is a good career move?\'94 Johnny quizzed. \'93I didn\'92t think you\'92d be that sycophantic, Sarah.\'94\
\'93Ah,\'94 Ortolan ventured. \'93I might have a solution.\'94 He paused.\
\'93Get on with it,\'94 Chip ordered him.\
\'93The new version blocked reverting to our bespoke version. But it didn\'92t block loading our diagnostics.\'94\
\'93Would that work?\'94\
Chip: \'93No.\'94\
Johnny: \'93Excellent idea.\'94\
\'93What would the diagnostics test?\'94 Chip pushed.\
\'93Incompatibility,\'94 Ortolan said.\
Chip realised Ortolan might be on his side. It would be unlikely the new software would completely match their old specifications. Each variance would be a trump card for Chip\'92s argument.\
\'93Johnny,\'94 Sarah said. \'93Haven\'92t you got anything better to do for Christmas?\'94\
Johnny looked at the disfiguring bulge in Sarah\'92s asexual outfit. \'93Maybe next year.\'94
